5. Dialogue Design

The dialogue must follow Alva’s existing visual identity.

The interface should remain minimal, modern, polished, and readable.

Appearance

* Use the existing dark theme and glass-panel styling.
* Use the application’s current typography, colors, borders, and corner styling.
* Center the dialogue in the available viewport.
* Dim the Home Screen behind it.
* Keep the title clearly distinguishable from the body text.
* Use comfortable line spacing and paragraph separation.
* Avoid large decorative illustrations, character artwork, or unrelated icons.
* Maintain a clean editorial presentation rather than an arcade-style announcement.

The story is the focus of the dialogue.

Layout

Because this is significantly longer than the existing information dialogues, the interface must accommodate longer content.

On larger screens, provide a comfortably sized panel without excessive line lengths.

On smaller screens, especially phones in landscape orientation, the panel must fit within the available screen space.

Use a scrollable content area.

Ideally, the dialogue header and close button should remain accessible while the story scrolls independently.

Scrolling must not cause the Home Screen behind the dialogue to move.

Respect safe-area insets and existing responsive conventions.

Do not create an oversized modal that clips important controls or forces the entire application viewport to scroll.

Motion

A subtle opening transition consistent with other Alva dialogues is appropriate.

Do not introduce dramatic animations or effects.

Respect the user’s reduced-motion preference.

6. Integration With the Existing Dialogue System

Alva already provides an InfoDialog instance through app.infoDialog.

Prefer reusing and carefully extending that implementation.

Currently, the information dialogue is primarily designed around one paragraph of text.

Support structured, multi-paragraph content without breaking existing callers, particularly the Discover Screen’s play-style descriptions.

A good implementation would allow the existing InfoDialog.open() method to accept either its current single-body format or an optional collection of localized paragraphs.

Render separate paragraph elements rather than presenting the entire story as one dense text block.

Use safe DOM text rendering rather than injecting the story through raw HTML.

Maintain the existing dialogue behaviors:

* Focus management.
* Background inertness.
* Escape and controller Back handling.
* Backdrop dismissal.
* Focus restoration.
* Accessibility labels.
* Navigation scopes.

When the dialogue closes, keyboard/controller focus should return to the “Why Alva?” button.

Opening and closing the dialogue must not create duplicate elements, duplicate event listeners, or stale navigation scopes.

Do not create a completely separate modal system unless the existing implementation proves unsuitable.

